 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "limerick_norse",
   "title": "Norse Limerick",
   "text": "Norse raiders sailed up the Shannon and around 922 built a base on King's Island, the start of the town of Limerick. In 968 the Dál gCais kings Mathgamain and his brother Brian Boru beat them at Sulcoit and took the town. The Norse stayed on as Limerick merchants, and in time as Limerick people.",
   "people": "norse"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE TREATY STONE. The Treaty of Limerick ended the Williamite War in Ireland on 3 October 1691, and tradition says it was signed on this very stone. Its promises to Catholics were soon broken, and Limerick is still called the City of the Broken Treaty.",
   "plaque": "limerick_treaty_stone",
   "title": "The Treaty Stone"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "KING JOHN'S CASTLE. Built for King John in the early 1200s to guard the crossing of the Shannon. It held out through sieges in 1642, 1651 and 1690 to 1691, and you can still see where the walls were mined.",
   "plaque": "limerick_king_johns_castle",
   "title": "King John's Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "ST MARY'S CATHEDRAL. Founded in 1168 by D\u00f3nall M\u00f3r \u00d3 Briain, King of Munster, on the site of his palace. It is the oldest building in Limerick still in daily use, and keeps a set of carved fifteenth-century misericords, the only medieval set left in Ireland.",
   "plaque": "limerick_st_marys",
   "title": "St Mary's Cathedral"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE HUNT MUSEUM. The collection of John and Gertrude Hunt, about two thousand pieces from the Stone Age to the twentieth century, shown in the Georgian Custom House of the 1760s on the river.",
   "plaque": "limerick_hunt_museum",
   "title": "The Hunt Museum"
  },
  {
   "x": 61,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE MILK MARKET. Market day has drawn the county into this square since 1852. Under the big canopy you'll find farm cheese, Limerick ham, soda bread, coffee and people who know exactly what you should be cooking.",
   "plaque": "limerick_milk_market",
   "title": "The Milk Market"
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "ANGELA'S ASHES. Frank McCourt's memoir of a poor Limerick childhood in the 1930s and 40s won the Pulitzer Prize in 1997. He went to Leamy's School here, now the Frank McCourt Museum. Not everybody in Limerick liked how he told it.",
   "plaque": "limerick_angelas_ashes",
   "title": "Angela's Ashes"
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE RIVER SHANNON. The longest river in Ireland and in Britain too, about 360 kilometres from the Shannon Pot in County Cavan to the sea beyond Loop Head. It drains a fifth of the whole island.",
   "plaque": "limerick_river_shannon",
   "title": "The River Shannon"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "ARDNACRUSHA. A few kilometres upriver, the Shannon Scheme power station was built by Siemens and opened in 1929, then the largest hydroelectric station in the world. It lit up the young Irish Free State.",
   "plaque": "limerick_ardnacrusha",
   "title": "Ardnacrusha"
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "THOMOND PARK. Home of Munster rugby. On 31 October 1978 Munster beat the touring New Zealand All Blacks here, 12 points to nil, the first Irish side ever to beat them. Plenty more people say they were there than the ground could hold.",
   "plaque": "limerick_thomond_park",
   "title": "Thomond Park"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE LIMERICK. Five lines, A A B B A, and usually rude. Edward Lear made the form famous in 1846. Whether it is named after the city, perhaps from an old party song, 'Will you come up to Limerick?', nobody can prove. The city is happy to take the credit.",
   "plaque": "limerick_the_limerick",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Limerick"
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "LIMERICK HAM. In the nineteenth century Limerick's bacon factories made the city's cured ham famous across the world. It was smoked over oak shavings and juniper berries, and boiled with cabbage for the Sunday dinner.",
   "plaque": "limerick_ham",
   "title": "Limerick Ham"
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "LIMERICK LACE. Brought here in 1829, this fine lace embroidered on machine-made net became a fashion all over Victorian Britain and Ireland. The Sisters of Mercy kept the craft alive when the factories closed.",
   "plaque": "limerick_lace",
   "title": "Limerick Lace"
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "LIMERICK, LUIMNEACH: 'the bare spot of land' by the river. WEST TO BUNRATTY AND THE ESTUARY."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "SOUTH TO LOUGH GUR, twenty kilometres or so. Stone circles, a lake with a sleeping earl in it, and the best picnic spot in Munster."
  }
 ],
